The '''2017 EFL Cup final''' was the final [[association football]] match of the [[2016–17 EFL Cup]] that took place on 26 February 2017 between [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] and [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London, England.<ref name="EFLSchedule">{{cite web |title=Key Dates |url=http://www.efl.com/about-efl/key-dates/ |publisher=[[English Football League]] |access-date=16 December 2016 |archive-date=16 August 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160816192004/http://www.efl.com/about-efl/key-dates/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> The final was the first League Cup final contested under the "EFL Cup" name following the renaming of The Football League to the [[English Football League]] (EFL).<ref>{{cite web|author=Press Association |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/nov/12/goodbye-football-league-hello-english-football-league-rebranding-announced |title=Goodbye Football League, hello English Football League: rebranding announced  |work=The Guardian |date=12 November 2015 |access-date=25 January 2017}}</ref> As winners, Manchester United initially qualified for the third qualifying round of the [[2017–18 UEFA Europa League]], but entered the group stage of the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League]] instead by virtue of their [[2016–17 UEFA Europa League]] victory, passing the League Cup berth to the highest-placed Premier League team who had not already qualified for Europe, seventh-placed [[Everton F.C.|Everton]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Peter |last=Smith |title=Which European competitions will Premier League clubs be playing in next season? |url=http://www.skysports.com/football/news/11095/10879198/which-european-competitions-will-premier-league-clubs-be-playing-in-next-season |work=Sky Sports |date=25 May 2017 |access-date=17 August 2017 }}</ref>

The match was Manchester United's ninth final in the League Cup, having won in their last three appearances and four overall, and Southampton's second, having lost their only other League Cup final in [[1979 Football League Cup final|1979]]. It was the second cup final played between the two sides, following the [[1976 FA Cup final]], when Southampton – then of the [[Football League Second Division|Second Division]] – beat [[Football League First Division|First Division]] Manchester United 1–0.

